WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.964
How Wi-Fi notices a fall.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.967
Someone just fell in this room.

00:00:04.387 --> 00:00:05.582
No camera saw it.

00:00:06.002 --> 00:00:07.367
Nobody wore a sensor.

00:00:07.787 --> 00:00:08.875
The Wi-Fi did.

00:00:09.295 --> 00:00:13.946
In twelve minutes you'll know exactly how, down to the line of code that rang this alarm.

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:17.192
First, the idea, with no code at all.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.940
Picture an older person who lives alone.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.643
They fall, and the phone is across the room.

00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:26.945
Cameras feel like spying, and a wristband gets left on the nightstand.

00:00:27.365 --> 00:00:30.779
So here is the question: can the room itself notice the fall?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.267
Think of a pond.

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:36.420
Drop a stone, and ripples spread out and bounce off everything they meet.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.192
Someone on the far bank can tell a reed is there, just from how the ripples arrive.

00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:43.895
Radio waves do the same thing in a room.

00:00:44.315 --> 00:00:50.907
Three small boards keep sending Wi-Fi messages to each other, and a body in the way changes how each wave arrives.

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:52.713
We never look at the person.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.648
We watch the ripples wobble.

00:00:55.068 --> 00:00:58.844
The radio's report of how each wave arrived is called CSI.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.059
And Wi-Fi is not one wave.

00:01:01.479 --> 00:01:06.343
It is 52 narrow waves sent side by side, like 52 radio stations.

00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:10.624
For every message, the board measures how loud each station arrived.

00:01:11.044 --> 00:01:17.295
So every message gives us 52 volume numbers, and about 33 messages arrive every second.

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:20.446
Each of those narrow waves is called a subcarrier.

00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:25.283
Now squeeze all those numbers into one wiggly line, and compare three rooms.

00:01:25.703 --> 00:01:27.154
An empty room: calm.

00:01:27.574 --> 00:01:29.664
Someone walking: busy all the time.

00:01:30.084 --> 00:01:34.778
A fall: busy, then one sharp jerk, then flat, because the person is lying still.

00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:36.584
That shape is everything.

00:01:37.004 --> 00:01:40.610
The whole program hunts for one sharp jerk, followed by stillness.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.227
Let's follow one real fall through the code.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:48.257
This is a real recording: twenty seconds, with one real fall in the middle.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:52.927
Each Wi-Fi message reaches the computer like a letter in an envelope.

00:01:53.347 --> 00:01:55.352
138 bytes in total.

00:01:55.772 --> 00:02:00.935
The first 10 are the address on the envelope: which board sent it, how strong it was, and when.

00:02:01.355 --> 00:02:05.046
The other 128 are the letter itself: the readings.

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:08.127
Here is the function that opens the envelope.

00:02:08.547 --> 00:02:13.283
The first line reads the address: board number, signal strength, time, and a counter.

00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:18.759
The next line takes the 128 reading bytes as small whole numbers, called iq.

00:02:19.179 --> 00:02:24.064
Then the readings are paired up: the first byte of each pair, plus the second byte times j.

00:02:24.484 --> 00:02:28.367
That j just means: these two numbers belong together as one reading.

00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:33.118
The last line keeps only the 52 useful stations and drops the rest.

00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:35.048
Why two numbers per reading?

00:02:35.468 --> 00:02:37.836
Draw each pair as an arrow on graph paper.

00:02:38.256 --> 00:02:42.906
A pair of 30 and minus 40 means: 30 steps right, 40 steps down.

00:02:43.326 --> 00:02:47.444
The arrow's length is 50, from the classic three, four, five triangle.

00:02:47.864 --> 00:02:50.146
That length is how loud the station arrived.

00:02:50.566 --> 00:02:52.508
The direction we simply throw away.

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:56.937
But the bytes arrive like a conveyor belt with no gaps between letters.

00:02:57.357 --> 00:03:01.069
So every letter starts with the same stamp, written A five, five A.

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:03.324
The feed function looks for the stamp.

00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:06.304
Anything before it is junk, so it gets deleted.

00:03:06.724 --> 00:03:10.478
If fewer than 138 bytes have arrived, it waits for more.

00:03:10.898 --> 00:03:15.570
Otherwise it opens that envelope, cuts it off the belt, and looks for the next stamp.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.980
Each opened letter becomes one row of a spreadsheet.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.088
This line keeps only the arrow lengths, the loudness.

00:03:22.508 --> 00:03:28.609
About 33 rows a second, for 20 seconds, gives 669 rows of 52 numbers.

00:03:29.029 --> 00:03:32.528
Every row is one moment, and every column is one station.

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:34.505
Some stations are broken.

00:03:34.925 --> 00:03:38.893
Think of a choir where a few singers never make a sound and a few just scream.

00:03:39.313 --> 00:03:42.172
The first line measures how much each column wobbles.

00:03:42.592 --> 00:03:44.234
That number is called the variance.

00:03:44.654 --> 00:03:48.537
The next line marks the columns that wobble too little or far too much.

00:03:48.957 --> 00:03:50.493
The last line removes them.

00:03:50.913 --> 00:03:54.774
In this recording, 11 columns go, and 41 good ones stay.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.888
Next, think of the equalizer on a music player.

00:03:58.308 --> 00:04:01.529
Slide down the very slow drift, like a room warming up.

00:04:01.949 --> 00:04:04.744
Slide down the very fast hiss from the electronics.

00:04:05.164 --> 00:04:09.047
Keep only the middle: wobbles about as fast as a human body moves.

00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:12.042
In the code, butter designs that equalizer.

00:04:12.462 --> 00:04:16.046
It keeps wobbles between 0.2 and 12 times a second.

00:04:16.466 --> 00:04:18.684
Times per second has a name: hertz.

00:04:19.104 --> 00:04:24.544
Then filtfilt runs the filter forwards and then backwards, so nothing slides out of place in time.

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:28.182
Now we have 41 lines that mostly move together.

00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:34.127
Picture 41 fans watching the same match: when something happens, they all lean the same way.

00:04:34.547 --> 00:04:37.512
So we listen to the lean of the crowd, not to each fan.

00:04:37.932 --> 00:04:40.535
The first line centres every column on zero.

00:04:40.955 --> 00:04:44.411
The next lines find the direction the columns lean together most.

00:04:44.831 --> 00:04:46.666
The last line projects onto it.

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:49.838
That leaves one number per moment: the motion line.

00:04:50.258 --> 00:04:51.986
This trick is called PCA.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.908
Now we look for the jerk.

00:04:54.328 --> 00:04:57.805
First, the line is rescaled so its biggest swing equals 1.

00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:01.361
That way a big room and a small room can share one rule.

00:05:01.781 --> 00:05:06.603
Then gradient measures how steep the line is at every moment, like the steepness of a hill.

00:05:07.023 --> 00:05:11.140
The rule says: steeper than 17.5 means a hard impact.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.803
At this moment, the steepness hits 17.63.

00:05:15.223 --> 00:05:18.999
A close call: only 3 moments in the whole recording go that steep.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.256
But a jerk alone is not a fall.

00:05:21.676 --> 00:05:23.127
You might just sit down hard.

00:05:23.547 --> 00:05:26.960
So the detector works like a careful smoke alarm with stages.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:28.105
Calm.

00:05:28.525 --> 00:05:29.720
Something is moving.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.185
A sudden jerk.

00:05:31.605 --> 00:05:33.013
Then: wait, and watch.

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:35.631
Only if the person stays still does it ring.

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:39.077
In code, those stages are a loop over every moment.

00:05:39.497 --> 00:05:42.313
If the line is steep enough, the state becomes impact.

00:05:42.733 --> 00:05:45.592
On the very next moment, it switches to checking still.

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:47.804
Then it waits, and counts readings.

00:05:48.333 --> 00:05:51.789
It counts 83 readings, which is about two and a half seconds.

00:05:52.209 --> 00:05:55.601
Then is still measures how much the line wobbled since the jerk.

00:05:56.021 --> 00:06:00.458
Here the wobble is 0.06, far below the limit of 0.2.

00:06:00.878 --> 00:06:02.414
The person is not getting up.

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:04.733
Alarm, at about seven seconds.

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:12.684
From the jerk to the alarm took about 3 seconds, a little more than two and a half, because the packets arrived a bit slower right then.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.039
Last step: how the alarm reaches the screen.

00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:17.783
Think of a restaurant.

00:06:18.203 --> 00:06:22.384
The kitchen does the heavy cooking in the back, so the dining room never freezes.

00:06:22.804 --> 00:06:25.663
In the app, the kitchen is a worker called FrameSource.

00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:33.102
It runs the whole pipeline we just saw, and passes every finished result through a hatch, with a bell, to the window you look at.

00:06:33.633 --> 00:06:35.681
When the bell rings, show frame runs.

00:06:36.101 --> 00:06:40.154
It updates the room map, two charts, and the status panel, one line each.

00:06:40.574 --> 00:06:43.540
And if the result says alarm, it shows the red banner.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.568
One honest detail: the little figure on the map is a drawing of the current stage.

00:06:48.988 --> 00:06:51.313
It does not measure where the person really is.

00:06:51.833 --> 00:06:52.857
Try it yourself.

00:06:53.277 --> 00:06:55.922
From the source folder, run the app in replay mode.

00:06:56.342 --> 00:06:59.350
It plays this exact fall on a loop, with no hardware.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:05.786
Then a challenge: in the fall detector, change the still window from 2.5 to 1.0 seconds.

00:07:06.206 --> 00:07:07.657
Does the alarm come sooner?

00:07:08.077 --> 00:07:10.338
And does it now ring when someone just sits down?

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:14.964
Before we finish, a fair question: why these numbers, and not others?

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:17.216
Some numbers are not our choice.

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:21.434
Wi-Fi itself splits every message into 52 narrow waves.

00:07:21.854 --> 00:07:26.056
That comes from the Wi-Fi standard, so every board sees the same stations.

00:07:26.476 --> 00:07:30.402
The envelope size is our own format: an address, plus the readings.

00:07:30.822 --> 00:07:32.848
And the message rate is simply measured.

00:07:33.268 --> 00:07:37.770
It changed from one recording to the next, so the code never assumes a fixed rate.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.622
It reads the real rate from every recording.

00:07:41.033 --> 00:07:43.764
Other numbers are choices, and each one is a trade.

00:07:44.184 --> 00:07:47.640
The filter keeps only wobbles as fast as a human body moves.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:55.569
The steepness limit sits between two real lines: walking reached about 16.7, and the fall only just crossed it.

00:07:55.989 --> 00:07:58.208
That margin is small, and we say so.

00:07:58.628 --> 00:08:00.441
The still window is the other trade.

00:08:00.861 --> 00:08:04.189
Shorter, and the alarm comes sooner, but sitting down may ring it.

00:08:04.609 --> 00:08:06.358
Longer, and help comes later.

00:08:06.767 --> 00:08:08.346
And some numbers are results.

00:08:08.766 --> 00:08:13.502
Three real recordings went in: an empty room, someone walking, and one real fall.

00:08:13.922 --> 00:08:19.767
All three came out right: no alarm, no alarm, alarm, about three seconds after the impact.

00:08:20.187 --> 00:08:24.646
One surprise: the empty room had the steepest spike of all, from radio noise.

00:08:25.066 --> 00:08:28.842
A separate check saw that the room was too quiet, and stopped the detector.

00:08:29.262 --> 00:08:34.680
And one fall is not a statistic, so the thesis reports each recording, not percentages.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.148
These choices were not invented here.

00:08:37.568 --> 00:08:44.053
Earlier research showed that human activity changes the radio signal slowly, so fast changes are noise.

00:08:44.473 --> 00:08:51.364
It showed that the signal changes caused by a body are correlated across all stations, so they can be merged into one line.

00:08:51.784 --> 00:08:55.560
And it showed that after a fall, the signal goes from busy to stable.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.521
Here are those sentences, highlighted in the original papers.

00:09:00.333 --> 00:09:03.320
Now a hard question: what if someone just goes to bed?

00:09:03.833 --> 00:09:04.750
Picture this.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.818
After lunch, someone walks to the bedroom and lies down for a nap.

00:09:09.238 --> 00:09:12.374
If they lie down slowly, the motion line bends gently.

00:09:12.794 --> 00:09:16.400
The steepness stays near two, far below the limit, and nothing rings.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:21.982
But if they flop onto the bed, the line jumps in a split second, then goes flat while they sleep.

00:09:22.402 --> 00:09:25.560
To the detector, that is a sharp jerk followed by stillness.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:29.158
That is exactly the shape of a fall, so the alarm rings.

00:09:29.667 --> 00:09:32.099
Why can't it tell a soft bed from a hard floor?

00:09:32.519 --> 00:09:33.799
Look at the first line.

00:09:34.219 --> 00:09:38.848
Each motion line is divided by its own biggest swing, so it always peaks at one.

00:09:39.268 --> 00:09:42.852
A thump on a mattress and a crash on tiles become the same size.

00:09:43.272 --> 00:09:46.451
Then gradient measures only how fast the line changes.

00:09:46.871 --> 00:09:50.796
So speed is the only clue left, and a fast flop looks like a fall.

00:09:51.216 --> 00:09:54.950
We checked this with made-up test signals, not with a real recording.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.076
The slow lie-down stayed calm.

00:09:57.496 --> 00:09:58.968
The flop rang the alarm.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.680
So how could we fix it?

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.188
Five ideas.

00:10:02.608 --> 00:10:05.915
First, mark the bed as a zone where a fall needs more proof.

00:10:06.335 --> 00:10:10.111
Second, put boards at different heights, to see if the body reached the floor.

00:10:10.531 --> 00:10:14.733
Third, wait longer and check for breathing, because a sleeper breathes calmly.

00:10:15.153 --> 00:10:18.823
Fourth, after a jerk, play a sound and let the person cancel it.

00:10:19.243 --> 00:10:22.635
Fifth, use the clock: stillness in bed at night is just sleep.

00:10:23.067 --> 00:10:25.051
We are not the first to meet this problem.

00:10:25.471 --> 00:10:30.570
An earlier fall detector found that lying down and sitting down can look just like a fall.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.174
And the breathing fix is not a guess: other research measured breathing with ordinary Wi-Fi.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.584
Last question: what makes this different from what you can already buy?

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:46.455
Watches and pendants detect falls well, but people take them off to sleep or to charge them.

00:10:46.875 --> 00:10:50.949
Cameras see everything, and that is the problem in a bedroom or a bathroom.

00:10:51.369 --> 00:10:57.065
Radar sensors need no camera, but they need special hardware, at a few hundred dollars per room.

00:10:57.485 --> 00:11:02.435
Wi-Fi products exist too, but their software is closed and sold by subscription.

00:11:02.855 --> 00:11:09.873
This project uses three boards worth about fifteen dollars, its rules fit on one screen, and it needs no training data.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.369
Research papers tell the same story.

00:11:12.789 --> 00:11:16.907
An early system saw people through walls, but it needed special radios.

00:11:17.327 --> 00:11:22.959
Later work showed that cheap boards like the ESP32 can read the same channel information.

00:11:23.379 --> 00:11:29.011
And one warning applies to all of them: if Wi-Fi can sense you, an outsider might sense you too.

00:11:29.433 --> 00:11:30.606
And the honest part.

00:11:31.026 --> 00:11:35.741
This is a prototype: one room, one real fall, and no tests with older people.

00:11:36.161 --> 00:11:39.873
The next steps are more recordings, more rooms, and a fix for the bed.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:46.416
Ripples, radio stations, letters, a spreadsheet, three clean ups, one steep moment, and a careful alarm.

00:11:46.836 --> 00:11:51.764
Plus a bed it can still confuse, and a cheap, open answer to expensive products.

00:11:52.184 --> 00:11:53.528
That is the whole idea.
